  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nAs of 2pm, there are currently some elevated showers moving over \ncentral and northeastern SD. These showers will be moving east and \nslowly dissipating through the afternoon into the evening. The \nshowers are being driven by a low-level jet moving over the warm and \nmoist air advecting into this area, and mixing in the atmosphere \ncould help to get the stronger southeasterly winds to the surface. \nThere is the potential for wind gusts to be up around 30-40 mph for \na bit this afternoon. As the jet dies off this afternoon, the winds \nwill weaken and showers start to dissipate. \n\nAfter the showers move out this afternoon, precipitation chances \nwill stay out of central and northeastern SD tonight into Thursday \nmorning. During this time, the moisture will continue to advect into \nthe state both aloft and at the surface. This will help to prime the \nenvironment for the next system to come in. A surface low will be \nmoving out of the southwest US tonight and into NE/SD Thursday into \nFriday, with a frontal boundary moving into the area a bit before \nthe low. Some models show a few scattered showers moving in earlier \nin the afternoon along this boundary while other models lack some of \nthe instability to develop anything along the boundary. In this \ncase, precipitation moves in along the northern and western side of \nthe low Thursday evening. The low then moves northeast through SD \nThursday night into Friday. The most current model ensemble runs \nhave shifted the track of this low system to the north slightly, by \na county or two, which has shifted the area with the highest \nrainfall amounts. This shift in the track will need to be watched in \nthe coming ensemble runs to see if it continues to shift the \nrainfall amounts to the north, or if they start to move it back to \nthe south. \n\nModel soundings show saturated columns of moisture through the \natmosphere with tall, skinny CAPE profile over most of central and \nnortheastern SD. These sounding profiles are a good sign that the \nenvironment will be able to support enhanced rainfall rates Thursday \nevening through Friday afternoon. Models hint that some heavier \nbands of rainfall will be possible Thursday night into Friday \nmorning taking advantage of the environment to dump the bulk of the \nrainfall. There continues to be variability in the models for \nrainfall amounts from a few different factors, such as the locations \nand track of the system, where the bands of the heaviest rainfalls \noccur, and how long the system sticks around some areas. This leads \nto a larger spread, over 1 inch between the 25th to 75th percentiles \nin some areas, in rainfall amounts and lowers confidence. The models \nhave been fairly consistent with the rainfall just barely glancing \nnorth central SD, with between 0.25-0.75 inch of rainfall \naccumulation expected. The models have also been consistent with a \nbullseye of rainfall amounts occurring over east central SD \nsomewhere, where 1.5+ inches expected. Right now, the ensembles are \nshowing a 90% chance for areas in a line from Pierre to Redfield and \nthen Milbank, and areas in east central, to get an inch or more of \nrainfall between Thursday afternoon through Friday evening and 50-\n70% chance for 2 inches. With these rainfall amounts and the \npotential for even more to fall in some areas, there is a slight \nrisk for excessive and heavy rainfall from the system. Since the \nground is fairly dry from the lack of precipitation, it should be \nable to handle a fair bit of water, even after the rain we had the \nother day. However, if the heaviest rain bands are slow moving or \ncontinue to move over the same areas for a while, the heavy rainfall \namounts could start to impact low lying and poor drainage areas and \npotentially even cause a little bit of flooding.",
